/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A HUD has four parts.
   - Parallel rays look as if they come from a very distant object, so the symbols form a virtual image at infinity.
   - Fighters use a refractive HUD: the optics sit under the glareshield and the combiner stands above them.
   - IFOV is the instantaneous field of view in degrees; D is the diameter of the exit aperture (collimating lens) in mm; L is the distance from the eye to that aperture in mm.
   - Conformal symbols are drawn where the real thing is in the outside world: the horizon line, the pitch ladder, the runway outline and the flight path vector (FPV), which marks where the aircraft is actually going.
   - γ is the flight path angle below the horizon; Vv is the vertical speed and Vg the ground speed, both in the same units (ft/min here, with 1 kt = 101.27 ft/min).
   - On a stable approach the pilot places the FPV on the touchdown zone.
   - An HMD puts a small display and optics on the helmet: one eye (monocular, like the helicopter sight of the AH-64 Apache) or both eyes (binocular).
   - The hard parts are mass (a heavy helmet strains the neck under g and in an ejection), fit, and tracker latency.
   - Answer. γ = 3.03° below the horizon; IFOV = 14.25° (±7.13°); the FPV is inside it with about 4° to spare.
*/
